const body = `<h1>8.5b — Convolution: Blur, Sharpen, Edge Detect</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Requirement 1: Box Blur</h3>

    <p>A blur replaces each pixel with the average of its 3×3 neighborhood. That
    sliding 3×3 window is called a kernel.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">Kernel (each cell = 1/9):
┌─────────────┐
│ 1/9  1/9  1/9 │
│ 1/9  1/9  1/9 │   Apply to every pixel
│ 1/9  1/9  1/9 │
└─────────────┘

Before:           After:
██  ██  ██       ▒▒  ▒▒  ▒▒
██  ██  ██  →    ▒▒  ▒▒  ▒▒
██  ██  ██       ▒▒  ▒▒  ▒▒</span></code></pre>

    <p>Build <code>boxBlur(int[][] img)</code> that returns a new image. For pixels
    at the border (row 0, col 0, etc), only average the valid neighbors — don't
    go out of bounds.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>You need to modify a new array (not the original) while reading from the
        original. Why? What would happen if you modified pixels in-place while
        reading?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Requirement 2: Edge Detection</h3>

    <p>Edges are places where pixel values change rapidly. A simple edge detector
    compares each pixel to its right and bottom neighbors:</p>

    <pre><code>For pixel at [r][c]:
  rightDiff = |img[r][c] - img[r][c+1]|
  downDiff  = |img[r][c] - img[r+1][c]|
  edge[r][c] = Math.max(rightDiff, downDiff)</code></pre>

    <p>If the difference is large (> 50), there's an edge. Draw a horizontal
    gradient (0 to 255 across the image). Run your edge detector. Where does
    it fire? Where does it stay silent? Why?</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>A gradient has no sharp edges — values change smoothly. What kind of
        image WOULD trigger edge detection? Draw a sharp jump (0→255 between
        adjacent pixels) and test. Now draw a checkerboard pattern. Which method
        is it — row-major or column-major — and does the edge detection pattern
        match?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Requirement 3: Heat Map Filter</h3>

    <p>Transform grayscale into a "heat map" where:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>0-63 → deep blue (print "B")</li>
        <li>64-127 → green (print "G")</li>
        <li>128-191 → orange (print "O")</li>
        <li>192-255 → red (print "R")</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Generate a gradient image, apply the heat map, and describe what you see.
    Now apply edge detection FIRST, then the heat map. Now what do you see?</p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement a box blur using a 3×3 neighborhood average</li>
            <li>Detect edges by comparing adjacent pixel values</li>
            <li>Explain why convolution requires a separate output array</li>
            <li>Combine filters (edge detect → heat map) into a processing pipeline</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-basics.html">2D Array Basics</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-traversal.html">2D Array Traversal</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
